# {{application}} process mini-monolith

Generated with `chenile-process-management`. One Maven reactor contains typed inputs/API,
worker services, configuration resources and an executable Spring Boot package.
It uses the Chenile process manager, JDBC work queue, durable outbox, Quartz and management API.

## Build and run

Requires Java 25 and the current process-management runtime artifacts (including
`process-admin-server`) installed or published at version {{chenileVersion}}.
These process-management enhancements must be built from the current framework workspace
until they are published; the version number alone does not imply Maven Central contains them.

```sh
mvn install
export PROCESS_MANAGEMENT_API_KEY="$(openssl rand -hex 32)"
# Enter this key in the React process UI; do not store it in frontend environment files.
java -jar {{application}}-package/target/{{application}}-exec.jar --spring.profiles.active=dev
```

Local API: `http://127.0.0.1:8080`. Every HTTP route requires `X-Process-Management-Key`
and `x-chenile-tenant-id` (e.g. `default`). The shared key grants administrator access
across all tenants. There is no default key; missing/short secrets fail startup.
The `dev` profile uses persistent H2 under `.{{application}}/`. Never use `dev` in production.

## Generated services and workers

{{#processes}}
- `POST /{{serviceId}}` accepts `{{inputType}}` and starts `{{name}}`.
{{#leaf}}
  `{{name}}Executor` implements the leaf work. The framework reports success or failure.
{{/leaf}}
{{#composite}}
  `{{name}}Splitter` creates typed child payloads; `{{name}}Aggregator` aggregates results/errors
  once all direct children are terminal. Children can themselves be composite processes.
{{/composite}}
{{/processes}}

Custom splitters/executors deliberately throw until their `doStart` methods are implemented.
They never silently succeed. Use `makeSubProcessPayload(childInput, "ChildType")` to emit
any number of each declared child type. Use the framework base classes for lifecycle reporting.
The generated common `ProcessResult` summarizes bytes, child counts and errors; replace or
extend it and the relevant worker generic output types for richer domain outputs.
All worker bean names follow the exact `ProcessType` + `Splitter/Executor/Aggregator` convention.

One `ProcessWorkers` BatchService routes every hierarchy level and restores tenant context.
A bounded background poller starts after application initialization. Disable it with
`PROCESS_WORKERS_ENABLED=false` if workers run separately. The built-in JDBC CommandLineRunner
poller remains disabled so it cannot block application readiness and Quartz startup.
Outbox on/off uses the same commands. Business effects are not magically exactly-once;
use `WorkerDto.dispatchId` for application-level deduplication and size leases appropriately.

{{#hasFileWorkers}}
### File example

File workers accept **relative** filenames under `PROCESS_WORK_ROOT` (default `./work`).
`fileSplit` divides bytes into stable per-process chunk files under `chunks/<processId>/`;
`fileRead` reads a chunk and reports byte count and SHA-256. This is working local file
processing, not an upload to an unspecified remote system. Customize the executor for uploads.
`chunkBytes` comes from the process definition's shared `config` (default 1 MiB, max 16 MiB).
An empty file emits no children and completes aggregation; a missing/invalid file reports failure.
Keep source files immutable while a process runs. Identical replayed chunks are reused;
changed chunks are rejected. Chunk retention/cleanup is the application's responsibility.
The work directory must be controlled by the application; do not let untrusted local users
mutate paths/symlinks while workers run. For separate workers, share the same work storage.
{{/hasFileWorkers}}

## Definitions and cron triggers

`{{application}}-configurations/src/main/resources/{{application}}/defs.json` contains framework
definitions for every level, including parent relationships and shared worker configuration.
Selected definition source: `{{definitionSource}}`.

- JSON mode loads the generated definitions at startup; dashboard definition edits are read-only.
- Database mode inserts missing rows into `process_definition`, then clears the configurator cache.
  Existing database definitions and administrator edits are never overwritten on restart.
  You can switch modes with `--chenile.process.configurator=database` or `json`.

`{{application}}/cron-triggers.json` contains `ProcessCreate` schedules and typed arguments.
Startup creates only missing schedules via the framework CrontabService. Existing schedules
are preserved. Seeds default to disabled; enable them intentionally after preparing inputs.
Cron names are globally unique, so use distinct names across tenants. Cron definitions must
use known process types, Quartz six/seven-field syntax, valid timezones and typed arguments.
Use the management UI to inspect/enable schedules, monitor trigger history and trace all child processes.
Keep one scheduler owner in a deployment. New schedules refer to definitions from this host;
do not configure a separate admin server against unrelated definitions/storage.

## Service registry

`registerInServiceRegistry={{registerInServiceRegistry}}` on every generated Chenile controller.
Services remain locally callable regardless of this choice.
{{#registerInServiceRegistry}}
The package includes the registry delegate and publishes metadata to `{{serviceRegistryUrl}}`.
Supply a reachable registry and a proper externally reachable base URL for production.
{{/registerInServiceRegistry}}
{{^registerInServiceRegistry}}
No registry delegate or remote registration is required. Internal workers invoke the local
process workflow; they do not make HTTP or registry calls back to their own mini-monolith.
{{/registerInServiceRegistry}}

## Production

Without `dev`, supply `PROCESS_DATABASE_URL` (PostgreSQL), `PROCESS_DATABASE_USERNAME`,
`PROCESS_DATABASE_PASSWORD` and the administrator key from your secret manager. Provision
the full process/trigger/JPA schema, outbox/receipt/work tables and management history migration
before startup. The host validates schemas; it is not a production schema migration tool.
The normal API host's PostgreSQL startup/security requirements also apply here.
Expose loopback HTTP through a secured HTTPS gateway; change `PROCESS_ADMIN_PORT` and
`PROCESS_ADMIN_BIND_ADDRESS` as appropriate. Do not commit secrets or real input files.
